* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* VOCPATH(filename {, dict.flag})
*
* The operating system pathname of a file named through the VOC: the data part, or the dictionary part if DICT is written in front of
* the name or the second argument is true.  The file is opened as OPEN opens it, so a Q-pointer, a multifile name and the other
* forms OPEN takes are resolved as OPEN resolves them, and the pathname is that of the file opened.  STATUS() is 0 if it was found
* and the error of the open if not, in which case the value is a null string.
*
* A PATHNAME IS GIVEN ONLY WHERE THE ACCOUNT COULD OPEN IT.  The program is internal so that it can ask the account's OS gate
* (K$PATH.OK) about the path it found; a file inside the account is always told, and one outside it only to an account that has the
* OS access gate.  An account without it gets a null string and STATUS() 3035, as OPENSEQ of that path would have given it.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function vocpathfn(filename, dict.flag) var.args
$catalog !vocpathfn

$include err.h
$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h

   set.status 0
   name = filename
   want.dict = @false
   if arg.present(dict.flag) then want.dict = (dict.flag # 0 and dict.flag # '')
   if upcase(name[1,5]) = 'DICT ' then
      want.dict = @true
      name = trim(name[6,9999])
   end
   if name = '' then
      set.status ER$NVR
      return ''
   end

   opened = @false
   if want.dict then
      open 'DICT', name to f then opened = @true
   end else
      open name to f then opened = @true
   end
   if not(opened) then
      st = status()
      if st = 0 then st = ER$NVR
      set.status st
      return ''
   end
   p = fileinfo(f, FL$PATH)
   close f

   * Only a path the account could open is told.
   * 18 Oct 26 Windows port - Linux asks kernel(K$PATH.OK, p), the S.63 OS-access gate, which is held here (no such key).  What stands in
   * for it is the rule an account WITHOUT the gate gets on Linux: a path inside the session's own account directory is told, any other
   * only to an administrator session (SDSYS), which can read it anyway.  The deviation is told to Linux.
   ok = kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)
   if not(ok) then
      own = upcase(convert(char(92), '/', @path)) : '/'
      here = upcase(convert(char(92), '/', p)) : '/'
      ok = (here[1, len(own)] = own)
   end
   if not(ok) then
      set.status ER$PERM
      return ''
   end
   return p
end

